    <h1>Install mpx</h1>
    <p class="date">MACOS &middot; LINUX &middot; ARM64 &amp; X86_64</p>

    <div class="tldr">
      <strong><code>mpx</code> is the companion CLI for Multiplex.</strong> You run it on the machine you want to add — <code>mpx bind</code> offers the machine to the app with a QR code in the terminal and an announcement on the local network, and enrolls a public key when you confirm. Prebuilt for macOS and Linux; nothing to install on your Apple&nbsp;devices.
    </div>

    <h2>Homebrew <span class="os">macOS &middot; Linux</span></h2>
    <pre>brew install multiplex-term/tap/mpx</pre>
    <p>Installs <code>mpx</code> and the spelled-out <code>multiplex</code> alias from the <a href="https://github.com/multiplex-term/homebrew-tap">multiplex-term/tap</a> tap. Later, <code>brew upgrade mpx</code> updates it and <code>brew uninstall mpx</code> removes it.</p>

    <h2>One-line installer <span class="os">macOS &middot; Linux</span></h2>
    <pre>curl -fsSL https://multiplexterm.dev/install-mpx-cli | sh</pre>
    <p>For machines without Homebrew — a server is exactly where you usually run <code>mpx bind</code>. The script is plain POSIX <code>sh</code> (dash and busybox included), needs only <code>curl</code> and <code>tar</code>, and:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Verifies before installing.</strong> Every download is checked against the release&rsquo;s published <code>SHA256SUMS</code>; a mismatch refuses to install.</li>
      <li><strong>Never asks for root.</strong> It installs into the first writable directory already on your <code>PATH</code> (<code>~/.local/bin</code>, <code>/usr/local/bin</code>, or <code>~/bin</code>) and tells you if none qualifies — no <code>sudo</code>, ever.</li>
      <li><strong>Installs both names</strong>: <code>mpx</code> and the <code>multiplex</code> alias.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Adjust it with environment variables:</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>MPX_VERSION=v0.1.1</code></td><td>Install a specific tag instead of the latest release.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>MPX_INSTALL_DIR=~/bin</code></td><td>Install here instead of the default search.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>MPX_NO_ALIAS=1</code></td><td>Install only <code>mpx</code>, skip the <code>multiplex</code> alias.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <pre>curl -fsSL https://multiplexterm.dev/install-mpx-cli | MPX_INSTALL_DIR=~/bin sh</pre>
    <p>Prefer to read what you run? The installer lives at <a href="../install-mpx-cli"><code>multiplexterm.dev/install-mpx-cli</code></a> — open it first, then pipe it.</p>

    <h2>Manual download</h2>
    <p>Every release on <a href="https://github.com/multiplex-term/multiplex-cli-releases/releases">multiplex-term/multiplex-cli-releases</a> ships four archives. Linux builds are statically linked against musl, so one binary covers glibc distributions and Alpine alike.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th>Archive</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>macOS, Apple silicon</td><td><code>mpx-&lt;tag&gt;-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>macOS, Intel</td><td><code>mpx-&lt;tag&gt;-x86_64-apple-darwin.tar.gz</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Linux, arm64</td><td><code>mpx-&lt;tag&gt;-aarch64-unknown-linux-musl.tar.gz</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Linux, x86_64</td><td><code>mpx-&lt;tag&gt;-x86_64-unknown-linux-musl.tar.gz</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Verify against the same release&rsquo;s <code>SHA256SUMS</code>, then put <code>mpx</code> somewhere on your <code>PATH</code>:</p>
    <pre>shasum -a 256 -c SHA256SUMS --ignore-missing   <span class="dim"># sha256sum on Linux</span>
tar -xzf mpx-&lt;tag&gt;-&lt;target&gt;.tar.gz
install -m 755 mpx-&lt;tag&gt;-&lt;target&gt;/mpx ~/.local/bin/</pre>

    <h2>Then bind the machine</h2>
    <p>On the machine you just installed to:</p>
    <pre>mpx bind</pre>
    <p>It prints a QR code right in the terminal and announces itself on the local network. Scan it with Multiplex, or approve it from <strong>Add Host &#9656; BIND</strong> and type the PIN; the machine confirms on its own screen and lands on the wall. Add <code>--copy</code> to put the offer on the clipboard instead, or <code>--addr</code> to name the address the app should reach when the machine sits behind NAT.</p>
    <p>Everything is reversible by name: the enrolled key is labeled in <code>authorized_keys</code>, so <code>mpx unbind</code> removes exactly what bind added — nothing else.</p>

    <h2>Problems?</h2>
    <p><a href="https://github.com/multiplex-term/multiplex-cli-releases/issues">Open an issue</a> on the releases repository, and include <code>mpx --version</code> and your platform.</p>
